[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What should actually be stored as preferences? (Pick all that apply.) → Remembered username[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Useful constraint: a remembered username has to be readable [3mbefore[0m login, so it can't be server-side keyed to the
  account. That rules out a backend and settles the mechanism as client-side [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m ([38;5;153msessionStorage[39m dies with the
  tab, and a cookie would ship the username to the server on every request for no benefit). I'll take that as decided
  unless you object.

  The one remaining question on this preference is consent, and it does change the design:

  - [1mAutomatic[0m — we persist the username on every successful login. Simplest: one key, no UI change. But on a shared or
    public machine the next person sees the previous user's username with no way to have prevented it, and nothing in
    the UI tells you it's happening.
  - [1mOpt-in checkbox[0m — a "Remember me" box next to the form. Costs an extra stored value (the preference [3mand[0m the flag), a
    markup change, and the clear-on-uncheck path. In exchange the behavior is visible and refusable, which is the
    conventional treatment for exactly this field.

  Either way the value sits in plaintext [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, readable by any script on the origin — fine for a username, and
  the password is never stored under any option here.

  I recommend opt-in: it's a small amount of extra work, and silently remembering an identifier is the kind of default
  people are surprised by.
